{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"0e1dff65bcd95087","name":"card_event_iron_victory","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/BloodIron/events/card_event_iron_victory","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"IRON VICTORY","text":"Discard your hand. For each card discarded, add a T-34 1942 with 1 operation cost to hand.","flavorText":"The Soviet Union produced approximately 105,000 tanks from 1941 to 1945, including self-propelled artillery, assault guns and tank destroyers. For comparison, Germany manufactured a total of 49,777 tanks from 1939 to 1945. Estimates suggest that the Soviet Union had around 30,000 to 35,000 tanks in service or reserve by the end of the war, while Germany had about 5,000 to 7,000 tanks remaining, largely due to heavy losses on multiple fronts and their inability to match Soviet production rates. The Soviet Union’s industrial mobilization and mass production capabilities allowed it to outproduce Germany in tanks and armored vehicles, which was crucial to sustaining large-scale offensives from Stalingrad to Berlin. This difference in tank numbers gave the Soviet Red Army a decisive advantage in the final stages of the war in Europe.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_iron_victory","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_iron_victory_title","SourceString":"IRON VICTORY","LocalizedString":"IRON VICTORY"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_iron_victory_text","SourceString":"Discard your hand. For each card discarded, add a T-34 1942 with 1 operation cost to hand.","LocalizedString":"Discard your hand. For each card discarded, add a T-34 1942 with 1 operation cost to hand."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_iron_victory_history","SourceString":"The Soviet Union produced approximately 105,000 tanks from 1941 to 1945, including self-propelled artillery, assault guns and tank destroyers. For comparison, Germany manufactured a total of 49,777 tanks from 1939 to 1945. Estimates suggest that the Soviet Union had around 30,000 to 35,000 tanks in service or reserve by the end of the war, while Germany had about 5,000 to 7,000 tanks remaining, largely due to heavy losses on multiple fronts and their inability to match Soviet production rates. The Soviet Union’s industrial mobilization and mass production capabilities allowed it to outproduce Germany in tanks and armored vehicles, which was crucial to sustaining large-scale offensives from Stalingrad to Berlin. This difference in tank numbers gave the Soviet Red Army a decisive advantage in the final stages of the war in Europe.","LocalizedString":"The Soviet Union produced approximately 105,000 tanks from 1941 to 1945, including self-propelled artillery, assault guns and tank destroyers. For comparison, Germany manufactured a total of 49,777 tanks from 1939 to 1945. Estimates suggest that the Soviet Union had around 30,000 to 35,000 tanks in service or reserve by the end of the war, while Germany had about 5,000 to 7,000 tanks remaining, largely due to heavy losses on multiple fronts and their inability to match Soviet production rates. The Soviet Union’s industrial mobilization and mass production capabilities allowed it to outproduce Germany in tanks and armored vehicles, which was crucial to sustaining large-scale offensives from Stalingrad to Berlin. This difference in tank numbers gave the Soviet Red Army a decisive advantage in the final stages of the war in Europe."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BloodAndIron","draftBaseWeight":0.8,"spawnCardName":"card_unit_t_34","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Mother_Russia'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Mother_Russia.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/Winte